# Data modes — demo vs full

A non-coder needs the app to work *now* and to persist *later*, without ever
configuring a database. Two modes solve that (the crm-builder pattern).

## Demo mode (default for building)

- Data is mocked / in-memory (e.g. a fake data provider, SQLite file, or seed
  fixtures). Reloading resets it.
- **Instant** — no network, no migrations, no provisioning. Iterate fast, preview
  immediately.
- Schema changes touch only the app's models/types. No SQL runs.

## Full mode (for real use)

- A real backend (managed Postgres / Supabase / your DB of choice):
  **{{ stack.database }}**.
- Schema changes require a migration (`.claude/skills/writing-migrations`),
  generated from the diff and applied deliberately.
- Real auth, persistence, file storage, and access rules apply.

## Flow

```
build + preview in DEMO  ->  user happy  ->  promote to FULL:
   generate migration from the schema diff  ->  review SQL  ->  apply  ->  deploy
```

## Rules

- Build features so they work in **demo first** — don't block iteration on a DB.
- Promote to full only when the user is satisfied (after the satisfaction loop).
- Switching a non-coder's app to full / real data is a confirmed step — say so in
  plain language ("Want me to switch your app to your real data now?").
- Never point demo at real data; never run a destructive migration without a
  human OK.
